# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 has "same_rulers → sedition" and "gold_fixed → same_rulers" and "gold_fixed" and "¬military_happy" and "city_happy → part_happy"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 has "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "sedition → hazardous" and "gold_fixed → same_rulers" and "¬military_happy" and "□(city_happy → part_happy)"; they differ when only "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "this arrangement becomes the cause of sedition", "settling the magistracy as Socrates has done is very hazardous", "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but always continues with the same", "the whole city is happy" and "all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy" are true, which the first allows; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 has "same_rulers" and "same_rulers → sedition" and "gold_fixed → same_rulers" and "¬military_happy" and "legislator_all_happy" and "city_happy → part_happy"
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S1 is read aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "should" or "can" does not say which it means
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "Socrates would have persons of the same rank always in office", "this arrangement becomes the cause of sedition", "settling the magistracy as Socrates has done is very hazardous", "the golden particle God mixed into the soul does not pass from one to another but always continues with the same", "the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy", "the whole city is happy" and "all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy" are true and "the military are happy" and "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)" are false. Is S1, "It is also very hazardous to settle the magistracy as Socrates has done; for he would have persons of the same rank always in office, which becomes the cause of sedition even amongst those who are of no account, but more particularly amongst those who are of a courageous and warlike disposition; it is indeed evidently necessary that he should frame his community in this manner; for that golden particle which God has mixed up in the soul of man flies not from one to the other, but always continues with the same; for he says, that some of our species have gold, and others silver, blended in their composition from the moment of their birth: but those who are to be husbandmen and artists, brass and iron; besides, though he deprives the military of happiness, he says, that the legislator ought to make all the citizens happy; but it is impossible that the whole city can be happy, without all, or the greater, or some part of it be happy.", true then?
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 has "happiness_individual" and "happiness_individual → (part_happy → military_happy ∨ artisans_happy ∨ multitude_happy)" and "¬artisans_happy ∧ ¬multitude_happy"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 has "happiness_individual" and "happiness_individual → □(city_happy → part_happy)" and "¬artisans_happy" and "¬multitude_happy" and "part_happy → military_happy ∨ artisans_happy ∨ multitude_happy"; they differ when only "the military are happy", "all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy" and "happiness cannot be added together but must exist in every individual" are true, which the first allows; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 has "happiness_individual" and "happiness_individual → (city_happy → part_happy)" and "part_happy → military_happy ∨ artisans_happy ∨ multitude_happy" and "¬artisans_happy" and "¬multitude_happy"
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "cannot" or "must" does not say which it means
aristotle-politics-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 and aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the military are happy", "the whole city is happy", "all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy" and "happiness cannot be added together but must exist in every individual" are true and "the artisans are happy", "the multitude employed in inferior offices are happy" and "necessarily, (if the whole city is happy, then all, or the greater, or some part of the city is happy)" are false. Is S2, "For happiness is not like that numerical equality which arises from certain numbers when added together, although neither of them may separately contain it; for happiness cannot be thus added together, but must exist in every individual, as some properties belong to every integral; and if the military are not happy, who else are so? for the artisans are not, nor the multitude of those who are employed in inferior offices.", true then?
aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2 has "sedition → hazardous" and "hazardous ∧ ¬city_happy → defective"; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.2 has "hazardous ∧ ¬city_happy → defective"; they differ when only "this arrangement becomes the cause of sedition" is true, which the second allows; aristotle-politics-1.logic.r2.3 has "sedition → hazardous" and "hazardous ∧ legislator_all_happy ∧ ¬city_happy → defective"
